Ddoc

$(SPEC_S Associative Arrays,

$(HEADERNAV_TOC)

$(LASER_REJECTED D associative arrays are not part of Laser-D. Both an
associative-array type $(D Value[Key]) and an associative-array literal are
rejected during frontend semantic analysis.)

$(P This is a complete rejection, rather than a restriction to particular key
or value types. ImportC's internal compiler data structures do not expose D
associative arrays as an ImportC source feature.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 rationale, Rationale))

$(P A built-in D associative array is a runtime-managed hash table. Insertion,
lookup, removal, growth, iteration, hashing, equality, and destruction can
require hidden allocation, runtime hooks, and type metadata. Those requirements
conflict with Laser-D's lack of the D runtime and garbage collector and with its
preference for explicit storage and control flow.)

$(P Programs may implement a hash table as an ordinary struct over fixed,
caller-provided, manually managed, or C-owned storage. Such a type uses explicit
methods and remains subject to the normal Laser-D rules for structs, pointers,
slices, allocation, cleanup, and ranges.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 declarations, Types and Declarations))

$(LASER_REJECTED The D type grammar form $(D Value[Key]) is rejected regardless
of storage duration, qualifiers, aliases, nesting, or whether a value is ever
inserted.)

---
int[int] values; // rejected
---

$(P Fixed arrays use an integral compile-time dimension and remain available as
specified by the arrays chapter. The syntax is distinguished semantically: a
type in the brackets denotes a rejected associative array, while an integral
compile-time value denotes a fixed array.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 literals, Literals))

$(LASER_REJECTED Key-value array literals are rejected, including literals
whose value might otherwise be used only during compile-time execution.)

---
auto values = [1: 10, 2: 20]; // rejected
---

$(P Templates, CTFE, and $(D static if) cannot restore associative-array
literals or types. Every ordinary Laser-D restriction also applies during
compile-time execution.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 removing_keys, Removing Keys))

$(P The built-in associative-array $(D remove) and $(D clear) operations are
unavailable because there can be no associative-array receiver.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 testing_membership, Membership))

$(P The D associative-array membership expression $(D key in table) is
unavailable. The ordinary scalar and pointer uses of operators are unaffected.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 using_classes_as_key, Class Keys))

$(P Classes are independently rejected in Laser-D. Associative arrays remain
rejected for all key types.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 using_struct_as_key, Struct and Union Keys))

$(P Structs and unions remain supported value types, but they cannot be used to
form a built-in associative-array type. User-written containers may define an
explicit hashing and equality policy without changing this language rule.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 construction_assignment_entries, Entry Construction and Assignment))

$(P Index assignment cannot implicitly construct or insert an associative-array
entry. User-written containers must expose insertion and replacement through
ordinary explicit methods.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 inserting_if_not_present, Conditional Insertion))

$(P Built-in conditional insertion operations are unavailable. Any equivalent
behavior belongs to an explicitly implemented container.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 advanced_updating, Entry Updating))

$(P Built-in associative-array update and entry-reference operations are
unavailable.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 runtime_initialization, Runtime Initialization))

$(P Laser-D performs no associative-array runtime initialization. This also
means that immutable associative arrays are unavailable.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 construction_and_ref_semantic, Storage and Value Semantics))

$(P D's associative-array reference semantics are not part of Laser-D. Storage
and ownership semantics for a user-written container are expressed by its
ordinary fields and methods.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 properties, Properties and Operations))

$(P Associative-array properties such as $(D .length), $(D .keys), $(D .values),
$(D .rehash), and $(D .byKey) are unavailable. This does not affect the
separately specified properties of fixed arrays and non-owning slices.)

$(H3 $(LNAME2 iteration-ops, Iteration Operations))

$(P Associative-array $(D foreach) is unavailable. A user-written container may
provide a validated Laser-D value-type range and use the ordinary range
iteration rules.)

$(H3 $(LNAME2 lookup-ops, Key Lookup Operations))

$(P Built-in indexing, lookup, membership, and default-value operations on
associative arrays are unavailable.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 examples, Replacement Pattern))

$(H3 $(LNAME2 aa_example, Explicit Container))

$(P A replacement container should make capacity, backing storage, hashing,
collision policy, insertion failure, and cleanup explicit. It may use fixed
arrays, non-owning slices, pointers, or C allocation according to the existing
Laser-D rules.)

$(H3 $(LNAME2 aa_example_iteration, Explicit Iteration))

$(P Such a container may expose ordinary methods or the validated struct-range
protocol. It cannot use $(D opApply) or delegate iteration.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 conformance, Conformance Boundary))

$(P Laser-D conformance requires rejection tests for both associative-array
types and literals. No individual operation needs a separate runtime test once
the receiver type is rejected, although diagnostics for syntax that constructs
a literal remain independently covered.)

$(SPEC_FOOTER)
)
